<header><p class="st-kicker">county · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">County of Tyrol</h1><p class="st-meta">1140 CE – 1918 CE (approx.) · capital Innsbruck</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An Alpine county controlling the Brenner Pass that became a jewel of the Habsburg lands and is today divided between Austria and Italy.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The counts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol">Tyrol</a>, named after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol-castle">Tyrol Castle</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merano">Merano</a>, appear around 1140 as advocates of the bishoprics of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brixen">Brixen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trento">Trent</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-iv-count-of-tyrol">Albert IV</a>, the last of this line, left his lands in 1253 to the counts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorizia">Gorizia</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meinhard-ii-of-gorizia-tyrol">Meinhard II</a> built a unified territory from Merano to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inn-river">Inn</a> valley.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol">Tyrol's</a> estates, including peasants, had a voice in government, unusually. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margaret-maultasch">Margaret Maultasch</a> handed the county to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> in 1363. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">Silver</a> mining at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schwaz">Schwaz</a> made Tyrol rich, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maximilian-i-holy-roman-emperor">Maximilian I</a> ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/innsbruck">Innsbruck</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landlibell">Landlibell of 1511</a> set out the estates' defence obligations.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol">Tyrol</a> remained a <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-habsburg">Habsburg</a> crown land, fighting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavarians">Bavarians</a> and French under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andreas-hofer">Andreas Hofer</a> in 1809. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, the south, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolzano">Bolzano</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-trent">Trento</a>, went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in 1919, while North and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-tyrol">East Tyrol</a> remain an Austrian state.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/county-of-tyrol.jpg" alt="County of Tyrol" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Nightstallion · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Tirol_and_Upper_Austria.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1140 CE – 1918 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Innsbruck</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Meinhard II, Margaret Maultasch, Maximilian I</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Bavarian German, Ladin, Italian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>, <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Tirol, Princely County of Tyrol</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1529&amp;focus=county-of-tyrol" aria-label="County of Tyrol on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4947 519 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where County of Tyrol lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5267 775L5229 694M5267 775L5381 747M5267 775L5269 749M5267 775L5380 747M5267 775L5274 792M5267 775L5263 812"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5381 747h0M5269 749h0M5380 747h0M5274 792h0M5263 812h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5267 775h0"/><pat